+//! The **multipart commit protocol** — the record family and state machine underneath
+//! `CompleteMultipartUpload` (proposal 0016, `docs/design/proposals/draft/0016-multipart-commit-protocol.md`).
+//!
+//! This module is the protocol, not the wire: it exposes the seven verbs — create, slot
+//! reserve, stage, part commit, fence+publish (Complete), abort, and the bounded
+//! teardown drain — over the [`MetadataStore`] / [`PlacementChunkStore`] seams. The S3
+//! verbs, XML, status codes and routing are the gateway's (#508); the scheduled reaper
+//! loop and every window-driven exit (`W_open`, `W_session`, `W_completing`,
+//! `W_tombstone`) are the custodian's (#625). Nothing here reads a clock: every instant
+//! arrives as a `now_millis` argument, so one clock owns each lifecycle
+//! (`AGENTS.md:131-142`).
+//!
+//! # Why this is not "store the parts, then concatenate"
+//!
+//! A client's uploaded parts are **durable but unpublished** bytes, which no pre-existing
+//! record class describes (`0016:155-200`). They must be protected while the session
+//! lives, evidenced for reclamation when it dies, bounded so a maintenance pass can
+//! enumerate them, and published atomically without ever exceeding a transaction
+//! envelope. The four invariants this module keeps (`0016:138-151`):
+//!
+//! 1. a chunk map is never published over bytes a maintenance pass may reclaim — proved by
+//!    **records plus a fence**, never by a timer (decision 1);
+//! 2. every durable byte is, at every instant, either **protected by a record that names
+//!    it** or **evidenced for reclamation** — so evidence always *precedes* the lifting of
+//!    protection (decisions 4.2, 5);
+//! 3. no lifecycle state is absorbing (decision 3, §2's exit table);
+//! 4. no batch is proportional to object size (decision 4, §3's batch inventory).
+//!
+//! # The records
+//!
+//! | Key | What it is |
+//! |---|---|
+//! | `mpuctl` | the fleet **admission ledger** — `{count, max_sessions, profile}`, CAS'd whole |
+//! | `mpu:<id>` | one **session**: target, state, epoch, attempts, publication |
+//! | `slot:<id>:<k>` | one **in-flight part slot**; the key space *is* the per-session cap |
+//! | `part:<id>:<n>` | a **committed part**: its chunk list, length and digest |
+//! | `psum:<id>:<n>` | that part's **summary** (no chunk list), written in the same batch |
+//! | `sidx:<id>:<n>:<chunk>` | one **owned staging entry** — a [`PendingEntry`] with `owner` **and** `staged`, under a prefix **disjoint from `pending:`** |
+//! | `retire:bytes:<token>` | a **retirement obligation**: bytes to orphan-mark, then the naming records to delete |
+//! | `retire:records:<token>` | records to delete whose bytes something else protects — **never** orphan-marks |
+//!
+//! Every prefix is disjoint from every other and from `inode:` / `dirent:` / `pending:` /
+//! `bucket:` / `orphan:` / `seg:` / `seggrp:` / `desired:dserver:`, and none is a prefix of
+//! another, so no `scan` returns a neighbour's records (`0016:333-356`).
+//!
+//! # Reading this module
+//!
+//! Every batch below is one row of 0016's §3 batch inventory (`0016:654-673`) and carries
+//! that row's preconditions. Two shapes recur and are worth stating once:
+//!
+//! * **the fence** — `require(mpu:<id> == <the exact bytes read>)`. It both authorizes a
+//!   transition and invalidates every operation that read the previous state, which is what
+//!   replaces lease liveness as the publication-time proof (decision 1). It is a *read*
+//!   precondition on the part path, so N concurrent part commits sharing it never conflict
+//!   with each other;
+//! * **the keyed release** — `require(slot:<id>:<k> == prior)` + `delete`, on the
+//!   committer's **own** key, which makes a slot release exactly-once by construction.
+
+use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};
+
+use bytes::Bytes;
+use serde::de::Error as _;
+use serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize};
+use sha2::{Digest, Sha256};
+use wyrd_traits::{
+    ChunkId, CommitOutcome, DServerId, FragmentId, MetadataStore, PlacementChunkStore, Result,
+    ScanPage, WriteBatch, SCAN_CAP,
+};
+
+use crate::metadata::{
+    self, ChunkMapError, ChunkRef, EcScheme, InodeId, InodeRecord, InodeState, ObjectMeta,
+    PendingEntry, RootPrecondition, SegmentBatchInfo, SegmentGroup, SegmentedPublication,
+    MAX_BATCH_BYTES, MAX_BATCH_OPS, MAX_ROOT_SEGMENTS, MAX_VALUE_BYTES,
+};
+use crate::placement::Topology;
+use crate::write::{self};
