diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
index 63182f6..0c7c079 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
@@ -21,6 +21,10 @@ The headline scenario; see `diagrams/write-path-sequence.mermaid`. Detailed step
 
 **Two linearization points, not one.** Introducing or moving a *name* (create, rename) is linearized globally by L2, so it is visible in every zone and never lost or resurrected (section 8.1); a file's *content version* is linearized at its home zone by the commit point. For a new object the global name is published only once its first content version has committed, so no reader anywhere sees a name without content. The exact interleaving is owned by the commit-protocol spec.
 
+**Writing an object in parts, over hours.** A client too large for one request uploads an object as an **assembled write**: it opens a session, sends numbered **parts** — often a dozen at a time, over minutes or hours — and only a final assemble step makes the object visible. Nothing about that shape fits the ordinary write path's proof, so the session carries its own record family. Admission is a **serialized reservation**, not a scan: one fleet-wide ledger record holds the live session count, the limit those admissions were checked against, and the budget tuple that establishes it, and every create compare-and-sets the record **whole** — so the count and its limit can never be read apart, a rolling configuration change cannot leave two hosts enforcing different bounds (a host whose own budget disagrees with the ledger's refuses to admit and alarms rather than deferring to either), and the fleet-wide population is *enforced* rather than observed. A session at the limit is told to slow down, which is a normal protocol answer. Per session, the parts that may be in flight at once are bounded by a **fixed key space** rather than a counter: a part claims one numbered slot before it streams a byte, so no amount of concurrency can create one more than the cap, the claim needs no shared writable key, and the reservation stamp makes a request observably alive from the instant it starts rather than from its first byte. Each in-flight chunk registers an **owned staging entry** before its fragments are written — under a prefix disjoint from the ordinary in-flight ledger, so no whole-namespace read of that ledger ever enumerates one — carrying the chunk's planned placement, which is what lets a record-only reclaimer address every position the bytes could occupy. A part that finishes replaces its owned entries with one committed **part record**, plus a small summary sibling written in the same mutation, **in a single batch**: the record that protects the bytes takes over from the entries that protected them with no instant in between. Assembling **fences** first — the session record flips to a completing state in one atomic step, and every part operation is preconditioned on that record — so a single O(1) precondition proves the part set is exactly what the assembler read, without a per-part precondition for each of ten thousand parts and without serializing concurrent part uploads. Publication is then the ordinary flip, and the version it records is recomputed from the **re-read** prior at each attempt rather than frozen when the fence was taken. Two answers are deliberately typed rather than generic: a rejected assemble list releases the fence and leaves the session usable and abortable, so a client's typo never wedges an upload; and a part, or a session's accumulated parts, past what the deployment can publish is refused with the staged bytes **compensated** — their reclamation evidence installed, their slot released — so a refusal is a bounded event with an exit rather than debris.
+
+**Cancelling, and cleaning up after, an assembled write.** Cancellation is one atomic fence that installs a durable **retirement obligation**; that fence commit *is* the response, so tearing down a ten-thousand-part session never rides inside a request. The obligation is then drained in batches bounded by **both** bytes and operation count — a batch of a thousand tiny mutations is inside every size limit while still spending the backend's per-transaction deadline, and a batch that always times out is stuck rather than slow — with the drain's cursor living **inside the obligation record** the same batch compare-and-sets, so progress and its record are one mutation and two drainers cannot both advance it. An obligation may name records in **two independent key spaces** — published parts and a rolled-back attempt's segments — and the cursor says which one it is walking, because the two are walked one after the other, each to its own exhaustion: advanced in lockstep they would stop at the first index absent from *both*, deleting the obligation while later records still named durable bytes. Draining is strictly ordered: **reclamation evidence is written before the record that protected the bytes is deleted**, so there is no instant at which a durable byte is both unprotected and unevidenced. Each piece of evidence is written under a precondition chosen by what the writer **read** there, because the mark carries the grace clock a reader depends on: an absent position is written and stamped now; a position already carrying **this** unreference event's evidence is left exactly as it is, so a second drainer racing the first cannot restart that clock; and a position carrying evidence from an **earlier** event is *replaced* with a fresh stamp, because that older mark may have expired long ago and keeping it would let the collector reclaim the bytes on its very next pass — giving a reader that overlapped the deletion none of the grace the window exists to provide. Which of the three applies is decided structurally rather than by reading the mark: a drain only ever visits positions at or beyond its own recorded cursor, and the owned-entry walk writes its evidence in the same mutation that removes the entry naming the bytes, so neither writer can meet its own evidence twice. The residue of a part that crashed mid-stream is reclaimed by walking that session's own owned-entry range, a judgment made entirely from **which records exist** and with no clock at all, and it runs for a completed session as well as a cancelled one, since a crashed part leaves bytes that no part record ever named. Only once that range is observed **empty** and no session-scoped obligation remains is the session record itself deleted — in one batch, preconditioned on the record's exact bytes, that also releases the admission slot, so a gateway draining inline and a background pass can both attempt the teardown and exactly one succeeds; the slot can neither be under-released for a live session nor released twice for a torn-down one. A **completed** session is deliberately not deleted at once: it survives as a tombstone that answers a client's retry with the recorded entity tag, keyed on the *identity* of the assemble request rather than on the session id alone, so a client that reuses the id with a different part list is told the id is gone instead of being told its assembly succeeded. Tombstones therefore keep holding an admission slot until their retention window elapses, which is why the fleet-wide count is described as the number of session records in **any** state.
+
 **Publishing a map that does not fit one value.** A segmented chunk map (section 6.2, step 2) cannot be written in a single mutation, so it is published in two staged phases: the segment records are committed first, in **budgeted** batches — each batch, and each record inside it, sized against the tightest backend's transaction *and* per-value limits, since a record over the value ceiling is rejected permanently rather than retryably, and bounded in **operations** as well as bytes, since a transaction of many tiny mutations is inside every size limit while still spending the backend's per-transaction deadline on sequential round trips — and only then is the inode root **flipped** to name the new generation. The flip is one batch that also carries the publisher's own mutations, so the publication instant and the publisher's state change land together or not at all. Both phases run under the **publisher's fence** — one declared record, pinned by every batch of both phases, since a batch pinned to some *other* record is overtaken by the rollback exactly as an unfenced one is: the rollback that reclaims a crashed attempt deletes that attempt's segment records, and the fence is what makes a stale publisher lose to it rather than flip a root over records that are already gone. The flip must also **move** that record, not merely pin it, or the racing rollback's own precondition still holds *after* the publication and it deletes the segments the now-live root names — and it must not move it **back** to a state the publication has already left, since a fence that returns to a value it held re-arms every precondition that was satisfiable there, which is the same deletion one step later; the rule therefore spans the segment phase and the flip together rather than each phase alone. And a publication refuses to flip over a segment range it cannot fully account for: a record left behind by a longer earlier attempt at the same generation, or a key in that range no resolve could read, is a refusal before the first write, and so is a range that does not yet hold **every** segment the root would name — publishing over any of them would report success and leave an object no consumer could ever resolve, with no prior generation left to fall back to and no crashed attempt for a rollback to find. Until the flip commits, readers and custodians see only the prior generation; a crashed attempt leaves segment records that its own epoch scopes and its rollback reclaims.
 
 ## 6.2 Read path
diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md b/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md
