@@ -82,6 +82,8 @@ A half-upgraded fleet is the normal state during a rolling upgrade (constraint 2
 
 **Metadata records evolve the same way, and one record's shape is load-bearing for it.** An inode's chunk map is either **flat** (the chunk list inline, a JSON array — the shape every record written before segmentation carries) or **segmented** (a JSON object: the segment-group identity `{nonce, epoch}`, a segment count, and the segment table). The two are told apart by JSON *type*, so a pre-existing record decodes and re-encodes **byte-identically** — which is a correctness requirement, not hygiene: every metadata compare-and-swap in the system is `require(key, encode(prior))` compared byte-for-byte against the stored value, so an encoding that gained a tag or a wrapper would turn every overwrite, backfill, reconstruction and rebalance of every pre-existing object into a permanent conflict. A segmented root is published only by the staged two-phase publication, which has verified that every segment record its table names is already durable; a committer that merely writes the root it was handed refuses the shape, because an object whose root is live and whose segments were never written is unresolvable for ever and unreachable for the rollback that would have reclaimed it. Two record classes accompany the segmented shape: `seg:<nonce>:<epoch>:<index>` holds one segment's chunks and the byte span it covers, and `seggrp:<nonce>` is a presence-only reservation marker that makes a group nonce unrepeatable. Both are **new prefixes** over which nothing older reads, so their introduction needs no migration and rewrites no record. The segment records are keyed by an independent group nonce rather than by the publishing session's id — they outlive that session — and by the fence **epoch** of the attempt that wrote them, so a rolled-back attempt's segments occupy a key range disjoint from any later attempt's and can never be reclaimed out from under a published object. Structural invariants of the segmented shape (the segment count, a gap-free ascending index sequence, a contiguous byte tiling that spans exactly the object's recorded size, a well-formed nonce, and chunk lengths that sum — checked, never wrapped — to the span each record declares) are enforced **at decode**, and by the same body at the **write** boundary — a record this system refuses to read is one no committer in it will store, since a stored record that nothing can decode is also a record nothing can overwrite, every overwrite being a compare-and-swap against bytes that must first be decoded; a malformed record is an error, never a value a consumer could half-resolve — and the error names the record class it belongs to, so a process that meets one contains the fault to the object carrying it instead of abandoning the pass, and with it every healthy object that pass was protecting. **Capacity** limits are deliberately not among them and are enforced where the record becomes work instead — the publication that writes a segment table and the bounded range read that would spend one both refuse a table larger than the object ceiling allows — so that a limit which moves can never make an already-published object *undecodable*, only unresolvable for as long as the limit stands.
 
+**A third class of durable byte, added without a migration.** Assembled writes (section 6.1) introduce bytes that are **committed to disk, referenced by something that is not an object, and not yet published** — a class the two-class world of "referenced by a committed object" and "leased garbage" had no place for. It is added entirely in **new key prefixes** — the fleet admission ledger, the session record, the per-session in-flight slots, the committed part records with their summary siblings, the disjoint owned-staging entries, and the two retirement-obligation namespaces — over which nothing older reads, so their introduction rewrites no record and needs no migration pass. One **existing** record gains fields, and it gains them the way the compatibility rule requires: the in-flight ledger entry takes an optional owner tag and an optional planned placement, both **omitted from the encoding when absent**, so a record written before the change decodes and re-encodes byte-identically. That is load-bearing rather than tidy for the same reason the chunk map's shape is: every renewal and every publication-time liveness guard is a compare-and-swap against the stored bytes, so emitting an explicit null would turn every renewal on every pre-existing entry into a permanent conflict. The owned entries deliberately live under their **own** prefix rather than in the in-flight ledger, because the fleet-wide population of them can exceed what a single whole-namespace read may return — and that read is one a post-restore reconciliation depends on, so admitting them would have made restore unable to make progress at exactly the scale it matters. Structural violations in the new records are errors at decode, never silently corrected defaults: a slot index or part number that is not fixed-width, a retirement key whose mode prefix is neither of the two, a session value carrying a field its state forbids. The **format** bound a key is validated against is deliberately not the live operator limit — an operator may lower the in-flight cap, and a decode that enforced the current value would stop decoding live records precisely when the teardown path needs to read them — so the current limit is enforced where new work is *admitted*, and every record written under any legal configuration stays decodable under every later one. One non-additive change of timing accompanies the family, and it reaches the **ordinary** delete and overwrite paths rather than only the new ones: removing the last reference to a *large* object's bytes owes one reclamation mark per fragment, which for a large or segmented generation is more mutations than a transaction may carry at all — a delete or overwrite that could never commit, rather than a slow one. Those operations therefore record a **single** obligation naming the generation and let a background drain write the evidence in bounded batches, so evidence is stamped **later** than an inline mark would have been — never earlier, which is the safe direction (a reader overlapping a deletion gets at least the grace it got before, and reclamation is if anything slightly delayed). A small object keeps the inline path unchanged, and while an obligation is installed and undrained it **protects** the bytes it names, so no instant leaves them unreferenced and unevidenced. The one shape without either route — a flat map so far past its own value ceiling that its chunk list will not fit one obligation record, which no conforming chunk size can produce — keeps the behaviour it has today rather than becoming undeletable.
+
 **Tested, not hoped.** Skew is exercised under the deterministic-simulation harness (ADR-0009): a simulated zone runs mixed-version nodes from a seed and asserts the commit protocol and read path stay correct across the gap. On-disk compatibility is pinned by conformance vectors per format version (`specs/conformance/`) that every reader must accept, and a mixed-version matrix in CI gates the one-version-gap guarantee (Q8) — the structural complement to the load/fault scenarios in section 10.
 
